---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BacCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/BacCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bac-calculator">
  <BacCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the BAC calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The BAC calculator uses the Widmark formula. It spreads the alcohol you drank through the water in
      your body, then takes away what the liver removes each hour:
      <code>BAC = A ÷ (W × 1000 × r) × 100 − β × t</code>. <code>A</code> is grams of alcohol, <code>W</code>
      is body weight in kg, <code>r</code> is the body water factor (0.68 for men, 0.55 for women),
      <code>β</code> is the fall per hour (0.015%) and <code>t</code> is hours since the first drink. BAC is
      in grams of alcohol per 100 ml of blood, the unit of US law.
    </p>
    <p>
      The grams in a drink are <code>ml × ABV × 0.789</code>, where 0.789 g/ml is the density of alcohol.
      A US standard drink has 14 g: about 12 fl oz of 5% beer, 5 fl oz of 12% wine or 1.5 fl oz of 40%
      spirits. The time until BAC reaches 0 is the peak divided by 0.015.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A man who weighs 75 kg drinks two 355 ml beers at 5% (14.0 g each) and a 150 ml glass of wine at 12%
      (14.2 g), 42.2 g in total. His peak in the model is 42.2 ÷ (75 × 1000 × 0.68) × 100 = 0.083%. Two
      hours after the first drink it is 0.083 − 0.015 × 2 = 0.053%, and it reaches 0 after 5.5 hours. A
      woman who weighs 65 kg and has the same drinks peaks at 0.118%, is at 0.088% after two hours and
      reaches 0 after 7.9 hours.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The result is an estimate for learning only. Do not use it to decide whether you can drive or are
      safe. The Widmark formula counts all alcohol as absorbed at the first drink; in real life BAC rises
      for 30 to 90 minutes, longer after food. The body water factor and the hourly fall are averages and
      vary a lot from person to person. Food, medication, liver health and drinking speed all change your
      real BAC. Impairment starts below every legal limit, and the only safe level for driving is zero.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
